### Why is sharding the last resort?

Sharding permanently changes the data model and introduces distributed-systems challenges that do not exist in a single database.

```java
package com.bank.sharding;

public class ShardingChallenges {

    // 1. Cross-shard joins are gone
    // "Show total balance across all accounts" now requires
    // fan-out to every shard and application-side aggregation

    // 2. Cross-shard transactions need 2PC or Sagas
    // Transfer between accounts on different shards:
    // BEGIN on Shard A: debit SAV2001
    // BEGIN on Shard B: credit SAV2002
    // Both must commit or both roll back → 2PC or Saga

    // 3. Choosing a bad shard key creates hot spots
    // Shard by city? Mumbai gets 40% of traffic
    // Re-sharding moves enormous amounts of data

    // 4. Global unique constraints and auto-increment IDs break
    // Need distributed ID generation (Snowflake, UUIDv7)

    // 5. Schema migrations must be coordinated across all shards

    // 6. Operationally: N databases to monitor, backup, and maintain

    // 7. It is effectively a one-way door: un-sharding is a major project
}
```

### What is the key nuance about read vs write scaling?

The earlier steps mainly scale reads, while sharding is the main way to scale writes. If the bottleneck is purely read volume, replicas and cache solve it without sharding.

```text
Scaling dimension:

  Step           | Scales Reads | Scales Writes
  Index          | Yes          | Yes (less I/O)
  Scale up       | Yes          | Yes
  Replica        | Yes          | No (primary only)
  Cache          | Yes          | No
  CQRS           | Yes          | Indirectly
  Shard          | Yes          | YES (main purpose)

Sharding becomes necessary specifically when:
  - Write throughput exceeds a single node
  - Raw data volume exceeds a single node's storage
  - NOT when reads are the bottleneck (replicas + cache solve that)
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should resist the urge to shard prematurely. Most systems never need it. The right time to shard is when you have genuinely hit the write-throughput or data-size ceiling of a single optimized, replicated, cached node.

```text
Senior decision framework:

  "The database is slow"
  → Is it a missing index? Fix it.
  → Is the machine undersized? Scale up.
  → Are reads overwhelming the primary? Add replicas.
  → Is the same data read repeatedly? Add cache.
  → Do reads and writes have conflicting needs? CQRS.
  → Is WRITE throughput beyond one node? NOW shard.
  → Is DATA SIZE beyond one node? NOW shard.
```
